      { q: 'What is the difference between standard BMI and Real BMI?', a: 'Standard BMI calculates body mass strictly using height and weight. Real BMI combines BMI with complementary calculator results to provide additional educational context. Real BMI is not a separate medical measurement or official WHO classification.' },
      { q: 'How is BMI calculated?', a: 'BMI compares your weight and height using a simple formula: weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared (kg/m²), or (weight in lbs / height in inches²) x 703 for imperial units.' },
      { q: 'How does BMI relate to population health studies?', a: 'Elevated BMI levels (25.0 kg/m² and above) are used in public health literature as a baseline screening reference for population health studies.' },
      { q: 'Why is waist circumference important alongside BMI?', a: 'Waist circumference measurements provide additional reference context regarding body fat distribution alongside BMI.' },
